# bob-client-setup.env.example
# Copy this file to bob-client-setup.env and fill in your values.
# chmod 600 bob-client-setup.env
#
# bob-client-setup.sh will source this file at startup.
# DO NOT commit the filled-in bob-client-setup.env to source control — it contains
# your API key (if provided).

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Full gateway base URL for your on-prem Bob deployment.
#
# If you have cluster access, get it from the Bob CR status:
#   oc get bob -n ibm-bob -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.gatewayURL}'
#
# Or construct it from the cluster ingress domain:
#   CLUSTER_DOMAIN=$(oc get ingresses.config cluster -o jsonpath='{.spec.domain}')
#   echo "https://api.bob.ibm-bob.${CLUSTER_DOMAIN}"
BOB_GATEWAY_URL="https://api.bob.ibm-bob.<YOUR_CLUSTER_DOMAIN>"

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your personal Bob API key (Scope: Inference).
# NOT required — users normally sign in through their IdP/SSO once gatewayUrl is
# set. Provide a key only if you want API-key authentication for Bob Shell.
#
# To obtain one:
#   1. Open your browser to: ${BOB_GATEWAY_URL}/admin/apikeys
#   2. Click "Create API key"
#   3. Give it a name, set Scope to "Inference"
#   4. Click "Generate" and copy the key immediately
#
# If left empty, no key is stored and you are not prompted.
# If set, the key is stored in the macOS keychain (service: bob-onprem-api-key)
# or ~/.bob/bob-api-key on Linux. You must still export BOB_API_KEY yourself.
BOB_API_KEY=""

# ── CA Certificate (Optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Path to the Bob CA certificate file (e.g. "./bob-ca.crt").
#
# NOT NEEDED for clusters with certificates signed by a trusted certificate
# authority (e.g., the TechZone showcase cluster or public/enterprise CAs).
# Leaving this empty ("") automatically skips the local certificate trust step:
#   https://bob.ibm-bob.apps.itz-3j2gqp.infra01-lb.dal14.techzone.ibm.com/
#
# Only set this if your cluster uses a self-signed or private CA certificate
# that needs to be imported into your local OS trust store.
#
# Obtain it via (from the install package):
#   ./bobctl get-ca-cert --output bob-ca.crt
BOB_CA_CERT_PATH=""
